{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1688 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "87ad5cbcbd548af53ff8e65fc3f458fb52fe074140c864f661401951692d5b02" :edb/db-before-t 1687 :edb/db-after-t 1688 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535000 50 #inst "2026-10-12T23:13:18.689Z" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124423 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\n- $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\n- $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\n- $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186124423 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447637 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124606 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447640 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447628 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447629 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447631 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447634 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447641 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447627 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447632 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447639 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186124733 1050 1080.0 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186125260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124160 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186125844 1064 "We can extend the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus to functions of multiple variables.\n\nFirst, let's recall the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus.\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\nf(b) - f(a) = f'(c) (b-a)\n$$\n\nNow, we're ready to present the **multivariable mean value theorem,** which states the following: \n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is a function that's differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{b}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\n where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively.*\n\nThe theorem naturally extends to functions of three variables.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n \nand the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 1,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 2,2 \\rangle.$ A sketch of this situation is given below.\n\n![](images/67/67c44030beb47bf9cc53a3550da39d3a467fe45445c722803a3a8eaf70f1cb8a.png)\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ we can apply the mean value theorem. That is, there exists a point $C\\in\\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ f(2,2)-f(1,-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨1,-1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting out the components of $\\nabla f (\\mathbf c)$ and expanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ {f}_{x}(c)+3{f}_{y}(c) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the above equation *must* be satisfied by some point $\\mathbf c$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}.$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186125844 1064 "We can extend the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus to functions of multiple variables.\n\nFirst, let's recall the mean value theorem from single-variable calculus.\n\n*If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that* $$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$$\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the above equation as follows:\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) = f'(c) (b-a)$$\n\nNow, we're ready to present the **multivariable mean value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is a function that's differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that $$f(\\mathbf{b}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}),$$ where $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively.*\n\nThe theorem naturally extends to functions of three variables.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $$f(x,y)= x^2 + y^2$$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 1,-1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle 2,2 \\rangle.$ A sketch of this situation is given below.\n\n![](images/67/67c44030beb47bf9cc53a3550da39d3a467fe45445c722803a3a8eaf70f1cb8a.png)\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ we can apply the mean value theorem. That is, there exists a point $C\\in\\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{c}$ such that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ f(2,2)-f(1,-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨1,-1⟩) \\\\ ({2}^{2}+{2}^{2})-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWriting out the components of $\\nabla f (\\mathbf c)$ and expanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ & =6 \\\\ {f}_{x}(c)+3{f}_{y}(c) & =6.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the above equation *must* be satisfied by some point $\\mathbf c$ that lies on $\\overline{AB}.$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198023 1071 "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $1 \\div (-7)$." 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198023 1071 "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $1 \\div (-7).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198023 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ by a negative one $({\\color{red}{-7}})$. So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\nA\n$$\n\nTo write down the number that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n1 \\div 7 = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-7)}} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{1}{7}}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198023 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ by a negative one $({\\color{red}{-7}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](images/b3/b3fdb7fe402f5e8ebfdf8502e8173faaaa11082ed9f618884cd1770e764ffaac.svg)\n\nTo write down the number that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n1 \\div 7 = \\dfrac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-7)}} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{1}{7}}.\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198024 1071 "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $(-5) \\div (-3)$." 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198024 1071 "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $(-5) \\div (-3).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198024 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $({\\color{red}-5})$ by another negative number $({\\color{red}-3})$. So the result will be positive:\n\n$$\nA\n$$\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5 \\div 3 = 1 \\,\\textrm{R}\\, 2 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198024 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $({\\color{red}-5})$ by another negative number $({\\color{red}-3}).$ So the result will be positive:\n\n![](images/cc/cce63c47aea469b99cccad529462e33e193ff094efb7d3535cf8ca095d8c23a9.svg)\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5 \\div 3 = 1 \\,\\textrm{R}\\, 2 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198025 1071 "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198025 1071 "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n$\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198025 1074 "A positive number can be obtained if we divide two numbers that are either both positive or both negative.\n\nSo, given a positive fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{-14}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the numerator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-3}})$. Therefore, the missing denominator must be $({{\\color{red}-14}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}-14}}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 false] [17592186198025 1074 "A positive number can be obtained if we divide two numbers that are either both positive or both negative.\n\nSo, given a positive fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{-14}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the numerator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-3}}).$ Therefore, the missing denominator must be $({{\\color{red}-14}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}-14}}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447630 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447633 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447642 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447644 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447636 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198382 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447638 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447635 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198383 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447643 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186198482 1050 420.0 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186199466 1064 "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}})$. So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\nA\n