624 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447627 1069 "q-105744" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447627 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447627 1072 #uuid "04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447627 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447618 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1069 "q-106255" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1072 #uuid "068c9cd0-bc02-5de8-abd9-1e9171a4647c" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447628 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,4)-f(1,1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,4⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩) \\\\ 0-(-4) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4y,4x,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{1}-8{c}_{3} \\\\ 1 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=3t+1,\\,z=2t+2,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=3t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=2t+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)+(3t+1)-2(2t+2) & =1 \\\\ 3t+3+3t+1-4t-4 & =1 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447609 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1069 "q-106260" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1071 "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1072 #uuid "0ee35382-ba28-5748-9984-ae6f2ae68bec" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447629 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447604 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1069 "q-161945" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1071 "$8÷(-3)$ is equivalent to" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1072 #uuid "19215bce-b998-5155-b240-d07f98b470fa" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447630 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(8)$ by a negative one $(-3).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n8÷(-3)=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nTo write down the number that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n8÷3=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n8÷(-3)=-\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447610 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447631 1069 "q-36808" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447631 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vect