d}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6.$ *" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447603 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1069 "q-36826" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1072 #uuid "cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447640 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447623 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1069 "q-106860" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1072 #uuid "db61b5b3-00ff-5d08-ac35-b46068ec6075" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447641 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447622 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1069 "q-161937" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1071 "$(-4)÷9$ is equivalent to" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1072 #uuid "dfe9429c-8eb3-53cb-93d2-56d808d34d21" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447642 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-4)$ by a positive one $(9).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-4)÷9=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n4÷9=\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-4)÷9=-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447606 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1069 "q-162136" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1071 "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{\\bbox[1pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-19}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1072 #uuid "ec1af280-a6fd-52ab-9d3d-511d96573c0d" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447643 1074 "A positive number can be obtained if we divide two numbers that are either both positive or both negative.\n\nSo, given a positive fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{-4}{-19}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $(-19).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $(-4):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{19}=\\frac{-4}{-19}\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447611 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1069 "q-161942" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1071 "$2÷(-9)$ is equivalent to" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1072 #uuid "fec8c045-97d0-52ff-98f7-547a00ead0cc" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447644 1074 "We have to divide a positive number $(2)$ by a negative one $(-9).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n2÷(-9)=-\\,\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nTo write down the number that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n2÷9=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n2÷(-9)=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447645 1062 #uuid "39822680-80da-5f81-9803-335e6ef154f4" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447645 1063 #edb/ref 17592186317389 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447646 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447647 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447646 1062 #uuid "6c9ca774-b4f6-520b-b234-3903b9ac3e3c" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447646 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222027 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447647 1058 #edb/ref 17592186447645 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447647 1062 #uuid "90354e30-ad99-522d-adc0-19a8645a92ab" 13194139535000 17592186045417 true] [17592186447647 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222036 13194139535000 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-34e3151a-47c8-57be-a129-fae3a26f1727" 17592186447476 "answer-009695df-eb16-5bf9-8f7d-15e791cfc73f" 17592186447477 "answer-016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" 17592186447478 "answer-01a312a5-7291-51cb-8dd7-289ffb878a9c" 17592186447479 "answer-0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" 17592186447480 "answer-039893a2-8d49-57cf-9a55-7abd0aa06682" 17592186447481 "answer-043c9e6c-3a11-5aaf-8219-a664d47467a2" 17592186447482 "answer-049f5769-5fd9-5a84-b0cd-5f4a4a5f43f1" 17592186447483 "answer-08d5e60e-7f26-5a6e-af43-a4123987b83e" 17592186447484 "answer-0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" 17592186447485 "answer-102ed920-ce8d-56fe-b777-875c7526e3cc" 17592186447486 "answer-152076bb-16b8-5594-b28f-fc29d1d41b72" 17592186447487 "answer-191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" 17592186447488 "answer-19da7377-89ec-5